# Lean enterprise sessions (مكّن)

Applies when the user pastes a long persona/system prompt, uses `[GENERATE-CODE]` / `[SECURITY-AUDIT]` / `[ARCHITECTURE-DIAGRAM]`, or asks for Trust / fingerprint / OTP / Edge Function / Zero-Trust work.

## Scope discipline

- Treat a persona prompt as **role + quality bar**, not a license to rebuild the whole platform.
- **One deliverable per turn** unless the user lists multiple explicitly.
  - Example: `[GENERATE-CODE] Edge Function` → only the named function (+ minimal shared helpers it needs). Do **not** also dump SQL, SDK, BFF, README, and fallbacks.
- Prefer **extending existing** `api/`, `db/`, `supabase/functions/` patterns over greenfield trees of `_shared/*` files.
- Cap new files: if a turn needs **>5 new files**, stop after the critical path and ask what to generate next.

## Tooling budget

- Diagnostics (`login`, `admin@…`, env, deploy): **≤6 tool calls** before a hypothesis; open the obvious auth/admin path first (`js/admin.js`, `api/v1/auth.js`, related schema). Do not re-scan the whole repo.
- Do **not** re-read every skill on every follow-up; reuse context already loaded in the chat.
- Skip `SearchConversations` / broad `Glob`/`Grep` unless the path is unknown after one focused search.

## Response shape

- Start with the artifact (SQL / TS / patch), not architecture essays.
- No fluff. Tables/diagrams only when the command prefix asks for them (`[ARCHITECTURE-DIAGRAM]`, `[SECURITY-AUDIT]`, `[COMPLIANCE-CHECK]`).
- Production-ready means **correct and complete for the asked surface**, not every adjacent subsystem.

## Anti-patterns

```text
❌ User: "Edge Function trust-challenge"
   Agent: writes 19 files (all _shared + verify + fallback + README + config)

✅ User: "Edge Function trust-challenge"
   Agent: trust-challenge/index.ts + only shared modules it imports; note follow-ups
```

```text
❌ User: "admin@mken.live logs in then kicks out"
   Agent: 14× Read across unrelated schemas

✅ User: same
   Agent: trace sign-in → tenant/owner binding → redirect; patch that path
```
